  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Treasure hunters dynamited them open and found nothing inside.",
        "visual_query": "costa rica jungle clearing",
        "visual_fallback": "jungle clearing"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In the 1930s United Fruit crews clearing jungle for banana plantations in southern Costa Rica started hitting stone balls. Hundreds of them.",
        "visual_query": "banana plantation costa rica",
        "visual_fallback": "banana plantation"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Over three hundred are known. The biggest is more than two and a half metres across, about fifteen tons, ground very nearly perfectly round.",
        "visual_query": "carved stone sphere monument",
        "visual_fallback": "stone sphere"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Naturally, somebody decided they were full of gold. Crews drilled and dynamited them, and several were gone before authorities stepped in. Solid stone, every one.",
        "visual_query": "tropical rainforest river delta",
        "visual_fallback": "rainforest river"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Diquis carved them from about 600 CE until the Spanish arrived, and the evidence is not mystical at all. It is architecture: spheres set in rows along the path to a chief's house, so you knew whose land you stood on.",
        "visual_query": "costa rican archaeological site",
        "visual_fallback": "ancient site"
      }
    ],
